      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's denote the cost of one pen by \\( p \\) and the cost of one pencil by \\( c \\). The given information can be translated into the following equations:\n\n1. \\( 4p + 3c = 224 \\) (since $2.24 = 224$ cents)\n2. \\( 2p + 5c = 154 \\) (since $1.54 = 154$ cents)\n\nWe will solve this system of equations step by step. First, we can use the method of elimination or substitution. Here, we will use the elimination method.\n\nFirst, we will eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We can do this by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 4:\n\n1. \\( 2(4p + 3c) = 2(224) \\)\n   \\[\n   8p + 6c = 448\n   \\]\n2. \\( 4(2p + 5c) = 4(154) \\)\n   \\[\n   8p + 20c = 616\n   \\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n1. \\( 8p + 6c = 448 \\)\n2. \\( 8p + 20c = 616 \\)\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[\n(8p + 20c) - (8p + 6c) = 616 - 448\n\\]\n\\[\n8p + 20c - 8p - 6c = 168\n\\]\n\\[\n14c = 168\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\frac{168}{14}\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.\n\nTo find the cost of a pen, we substitute \\( c = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations. Let's use the first equation:\n\\[\n4p + 3(12) = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 36 = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 224 - 36\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 188\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{188}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\np = 47\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is \\(\\boxed{47}\\) cents. However, we already found the cost of a pencil to be \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents, and since the problem asks for the cost of a pencil, the answer is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
