      "text": "Let \\( p \\) represent the cost of one pen in cents, and \\( q \\) represent the cost of one pencil in cents. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost \\$2.24. Since \\$2.24 is equivalent to 224 cents, we can write the first equation as:\n   \\[\n   4p + 3q = 224\n   \\]\n\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost \\$1.54. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   2p + 5q = 154\n   \\]\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224 \\\\\n2p + 5q = 154\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We do this by multiplying the second equation by 2:\n\\[\n2(2p + 5q) = 2(154)\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224 \\\\\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[\n(4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 308 - 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q - 4p - 3q = 84\n\\]\n\\[\n7q = 84\n\\]\n\\[\nq = \\frac{84}{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nq = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is 12 cents. To find the cost of a pen, we substitute \\( q = 12 \\) back into one of the original equations. We use the first equation:\n\\[\n4p + 3(12) = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 36 = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 224 - 36\n\\]\n\\[\n4p = 188\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{188}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\np = 47\n\\]\n\nThus, the cost of a pen is 47 cents. The cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
