      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the cost of a pencil in cents. Let's denote the cost of a pen by \\( p \\) cents and the cost of a pencil by \\( c \\) cents. We are given two equations based on the information provided:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost $2.24, which is equivalent to 224 cents.\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost $1.54, which is equivalent to 154 cents.\n\nWe can translate these statements into the following system of equations:\n\\[\n4p + 3c = 224 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2p + 5c = 154 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) in both equations the same. We can do this by multiplying equation (2) by 2:\n\\[\n2(2p + 5c) = 2(154)\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10c = 308 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n4p + 3c = 224 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10c = 308 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract equation (1) from equation (3):\n\\[\n(4p + 10c) - (4p + 3c) = 308 - 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10c - 4p - 3c = 84\n\\]\n\\[\n7c = 84\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\frac{84}{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. To find the cost of a pen, we substitute \\( c = 12 \\) back into equation (2):\n\\[\n2p + 5(12) = 154\n\\]\n\\[\n2p + 60 = 154\n\\]\n\\[\n2p = 154 - 60\n\\]\n\\[\n2p = 94\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{94}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\np = 47\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
