      "text": "Let's denote the cost of a pen as \\( p \\) dollars and the cost of a pencil as \\( q \\) dollars. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. \\( 4p + 3q = 2.24 \\)\n2. \\( 2p + 5q = 1.54 \\)\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables by using the method of elimination. To do this, we need to make the coefficients of \\( p \\) the same in both equations. We can achieve this by multiplying the second equation by 2:\n\n\\[ 2(2p + 5q) = 2(1.54) \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10q = 3.08 \\]\n\nNow we have the system of equations:\n1. \\( 4p + 3q = 2.24 \\)\n2. \\( 4p + 10q = 3.08 \\)\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\n\\[ (4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 3.08 - 2.24 \\]\n\\[ 4p + 10q - 4p - 3q = 0.84 \\]\n\\[ 7q = 0.84 \\]\n\\[ q = \\frac{0.84}{7} \\]\n\\[ q = 0.12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 0.12 \\) dollars, which is equivalent to 12 cents.\n\nTo verify our solution, we can substitute \\( q = 0.12 \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( p \\). Let's use the second equation:\n\n\\[ 2p + 5(0.12) = 1.54 \\]\n\\[ 2p + 0.60 = 1.54 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 1.54 - 0.60 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 0.94 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{0.94}{2} \\]\n\\[ p = 0.47 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is \\( 0.47 \\) dollars, which is equivalent to 47 cents. Now, we check the first equation to ensure our values are correct:\n\n\\[ 4(0.47) + 3(0.12) = 1.88 + 0.36 = 2.24 \\]\n\nThe values satisfy both equations, confirming our solution is correct. Therefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
